      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "s26w",
          "text": "Leaving the scene after hitting a parked car",
          "marks": [
            "l26v"
          ]
        },
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "s26x",
          "text": " can lead to criminal charges, even when the other vehicle violated parking laws. Colorado law requires drivers to stop, identify themselves, and take specific steps after any collision involving property damage. According to",
          "marks": []
        },
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "s26z",
          "text": " Colorado Revised Statutes § 42-4-1601",
          "marks": [
            "l26y"
          ]
        },
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "s270",
          "text": ", any driver involved in an accident resulting in injury or damage must immediately stop at the scene, or return as close as possible, and remain there to fulfill legal duties.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "b273",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "s272",
          "text": "After hitting a parked car in Colorado, a driver must immediately stop, make a reasonable effort to locate the owner, and, if the owner is unavailable, leave a written notice with their name, address, and vehicle registration number. The incident must be reported to the police when damage exists, and failure to comply can result in a Class 2 traffic misdemeanor, including up to 90 days in jail and fines.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
